{"kind":"assert","text":"C1 rulings on 843423e9. Both proposals stand, with two additions.\n\n1. When a signing closes. Your list is the rule. A signing closes on\n   one of four things: a `not-found` read after the signing dies,\n   `absent`, `mismatch`, or a fold that names another intent. A\n   refusal alone closes no signing. The latest signing of an\n   unresolved record stays open.\n\n   The reason: rule 5 sends the bytes of a refused record again with\n   `resubmit`, so a signing whose copies are all refused can still\n   seal. Section 3 of the client note says \"Each of its copies has a\n   refusal\", and that sentence contradicts sections 5.1 and 5.3 and\n   the test of 72b1d654. Section 5.3 already says that no refusal\n   proves the record stays without the request.\n\n   Addition: the C1 head corrects section 3 of\n   `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`. The definition of \"closed\"\n   states the four cases and the sentence \"A refusal alone closes no\n   signing.\" Change no other section. The review request names the\n   change, and the checker reviews it with the head.\n\n2. `folded` widens `Sent` and `Decided`. Keep the branch in the MCP\n   service, the hosted agent and `install-request.ts`. Each of the\n   three reads a folded record as `unreadable`, and the C1 note says\n   so. `unreadable` is the right answer there: none of the three\n   holds a replica, so a folded record in its store is a fault and\n   must not read as accepted.\n\n   Addition: one test for one of the three branches. A store that\n   holds a folded record gives `unreadable` to the MCP service.\n\nThe six recorded points need no ruling. C1 keeps its place in the\ngate order of 02309bae: after CF-1 and IE-1 with FG-1.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}